// The bench — docs/GUI.md §7. The only surface the program can open on, because there is nowhere
// else.
//
// Three things, in the order the eye meets them: **the well** (full bleed, `bg-sunken`, holding one
// device and nothing else, ever), **the device** (pinned by its distance above the bottom edge,
// never centred), and **the cradle** (the fixture around it, which is where every piece of UI
// state lives).
//
// **There is no fourth.** §7.5's shelf held three rows, ended holding one, and is deleted: its
// name-and-state row is the caption above the device, its write-target row is the caption below
// it, and the device summary and `k` it had left are `About this iPod` on the drawer's root page
// (§21.3) and §12.9's own argument against itself. The 44 px went back to the column.
//
// ── The two rules that shape this file ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **No UI state is drawn on the object** (§6.5, principle 3). Not on the centre button, not on the
// glass, not on the chassis. The accent annotates the *fixture* — a screenshot of the drawn iPod
// has to stay a picture of an iPod, and a glossy blue disc is a widget stuck to a photograph. That
// is what the cradle is for and it is the whole reason it exists.
//
// **Nothing that animates may feed the Screen's geometry** (§8.2 rule 1). `hero` is pushed in from
// Rust and is never the target of an `animate` block — and since §21.2 **nothing on this surface
// animates at all**: the one property that used to was the **x** of the frame holding the device
// and its cradle, and it moved because the well got narrower under a drawer that pushed. The
// drawer overlays now, the well is always the full client width, and the device is at the same
// place whether the drawer is open or shut. The shipped carousel animated
// `body-height` from `hero × 0.55` to `hero` over 320 ms, and every dimension of the drawing is a
// fraction of it — so a live framebuffer was drawn at a continuously varying non-integer scale for
// the whole animation. That is deleted here rather than reproduced.
//
// ── What is deliberately not here ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The `why ›` route's destination**, which is a drawer page. §11.4's drop band is here: its
// count and its *let go* are the caption's first line, its identifications are the second.
//
// ── §9.5's pane, which IS here now ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `ShortPane` below replaces the well on a display too short to draw the panel at 1:1. It
// **replaces** rather than shrinks: §9.6's whole vertical budget is fixed rows, nothing gives, and
// a squeezed column is the 560 px bug again — so below the threshold the device, the cradle and
// the cradle label are simply not drawn, and what stands in their place is three things that fit
// inside `MIN_HEIGHT`.
//
// The well still **clips**, and that is not the pane and never was: clipping buys that a caption
// laid out past the bottom edge is clipped rather than drawn over the edge of the client, which is
// a strictly better failure than the shipped one and no kind of answer to *there is no way to
// start an iPod on this display*.

import { Ink, Metric, Motion } from "tokens.slint";
import { Geometry } from "@geometry";
import { IPod } from "ipod.slint";
// **`CradleRing` moved to the leaf and is re-exported from here.** §7.3's ring is now decided in
// Rust — `machine::cradle` returns it — so it has to be a field of `DeviceRow`, and `DeviceRow`
// lives in `primitives.slint`, which this file imports. Declaring the enum here and the struct
// there would be a cycle. Re-exported so every existing importer still names one place.
import { Act, Pressable, CradleRing } from "primitives.slint";
import { DrawerPage } from "drawer.slint";
export { CradleRing } from "primitives.slint";

// ─── The two drawn marks this file needs (§6.7) ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **No icon is a font glyph.** Nothing in `.slint` can ask whether a font has a glyph, runtime font
// registration is behind an unstable feature, and a missing one falls to `.notdef` — which is how
// twelve empty squares once shipped to the operator. So `›` and `·` are drawn.

component Chevron inherits Rectangle {
    in property <color> ink;
    width: Metric.s3;
    Path {
        x: 0;
        y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
        width: 100%;
        height: Metric.s3;
        viewbox-width: 12;
        viewbox-height: 12;
        fill: root.ink;
        commands: "M 4 1.5 L 9 6 L 4 10.5 L 2.5 9.2 L 6 6 L 2.5 2.8 Z";
    }
}


// ─── §9.5's replacement pane ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **The display is too short to show the panel at 1:1.** A designed state, not an overflow, and
// reachable on hardware people own: 1280 x 800 and 1366 x 768 can never satisfy it at any scale
// factor, because the body alone needs 656 physical pixels and neither display has 810 of usable
// window height.
//
// Three things, in the order the eye meets them, and every one of them fixed:
//
//   1. **the measurement**, which is the only sentence in this program that names a number the
//      window is standing in right now — `main::short_pane` words it and pushes it live;
//   2. **the paragraph**, which says the drawing is withheld rather than broken, and says where
//      the press went;
//   3. **the primary Row**, which is the whole point of this state and the previous revision did
//      not have it. The well is the only thing that contains the drawn iPod and the drawn iPod's
//      centre button is the only start affordance in the entire program — so replacing the well
//      with a paragraph left §10.4's *bench with no step one*: the window still advertising a
//      centre button that is not drawn, and the one offered control (`Fullscreen`) disabled by
//      §12.6 in exactly the state §9.5 offered it in. This Row carries the cradle's own callback
//      and the cradle's own sentence.
//
// **`Fullscreen` is deliberately absent rather than present and dead**, and that is §9.5's own
// rule evaluated against this build rather than a gap in it: fullscreen is offered *only when the
// glass has something on it* — Booting, Running, Stopped, or parked with a frame — and §12.2 puts
// this build in `Off` and nowhere else. A control offered and refused in the same breath is worse
// than one that is not there.
//
// **`Metric.s4` between the three, `Geometry.well-air` around them**, and the measure is
// `Geometry.short-measure` — the widest line that cannot overflow the narrowest well this window
// has, which since §21.2 is the narrowest *window*: the drawer overlays, so the well never gives
// 420 px up and the two are the same number. It does not grow with the well; a sentence set
// across 1140 px is a sentence nobody can read a second line of.
component ShortPane inherits Rectangle {
    in property <string> headline;
    in property <string> body;
    // §7.3's caption, worded for a surface with no centre button — `main::Press`. One tail.
    in property <string> press-label;
    // §7.4, and it is the announcement rather than the gate: the press fires in every state,
    // because Rust is the only thing that can say which part is gone, and an assistive technology
    // is told which of the two states this is in.
    in property <bool> startable: true;

    callback pressed();

    // §9.5: the only start affordance on a 1280 x 800 display, so the window has to be able to
    // hand it focus the way it hands the cradle focus.
    public function take-focus() {
        press.take-focus();
    }

    // The well's own surface, because this IS the well while it is drawn (§7.1) — and it clips for
    // the same reason the well does: this pane replaces a column laid out for a taller window, and
    // a sentence set past the bottom edge is clipped rather than drawn over the client's edge.
    background: Ink.bg-sunken;
    clip: true;
    accessible-role: AccessibleRole.groupbox;
    accessible-label: root.headline;

    VerticalLayout {
        alignment: center;
        spacing: Metric.s4;
        padding-left: Geometry.well-air;
        padding-right: Geometry.well-air;
        padding-top: Geometry.well-air;
        padding-bottom: Geometry.well-air;

        HorizontalLayout {
            alignment: center;

            Text {
                width: Geometry.short-measure;
                text: root.headline;
                font-size: Metric.body-size;
                font-weight: Metric.weight-strong;
                color: Ink.fg;
                horizontal-alignment: center;
                wrap: word-wrap;
            }
        }

        HorizontalLayout {
            alignment: center;

            Text {
                width: Geometry.short-measure;
                text: root.body;
                font-size: Metric.body-size;
                font-weight: Metric.weight-body;
                color: Ink.fg-dim;
                horizontal-alignment: center;
                wrap: word-wrap;
            }
        }

        HorizontalLayout {
            alignment: center;

            // §6.5 use 3: *the one primary row on the too-short bench*. The material was written
            // for three things and this is the third of them.
            press := Pressable {
                width: Geometry.short-measure;
                label: root.press-label;
                primary: true;
                announce-enabled: root.startable;
                pad: Metric.s3;
                activated => {
                    root.pressed();
                }

                Text {
                    text: root.press-label;
                    font-size: Metric.body-size;
                    font-weight: Metric.weight-strong;
                    color: Ink.on-material;
                    horizontal-alignment: center;
                    vertical-alignment: center;
                    overflow: elide;
                    horizontal-stretch: 1;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// ─── A control on a caption line ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// **Not `Pressable`.** That primitive is a 44 px Row with a 34 px reason slot reserved under it,
// and a caption line is 16 to 26 px of a column that is already fully spent — there is nowhere for
// a reason slot to be. Its one use is also never disabled: `why ›` is drawn only when there IS a
// refusal to explain, so the slot would have nothing to hold.
//
// §16.5 all the same, because the trap is the same: **never `enabled:` on the `TouchArea` or the
// `FocusScope`.** A disabled `TouchArea` forcibly clears `has_hover` and forwards the event to
// whatever is underneath; a disabled `FocusScope` refuses focus even programmatically.
component BenchControl inherits Rectangle {
    in property <string> a11y-label;
    in property <string> a11y-description;
    callback activated();

    // §8.1 item 9: the focus ring is **instant, always**. A focus ring that animates is a focus
    // ring you lose.
    Rectangle {
        x: 0;
        y: 0;
        width: 100%;
        height: 100%;
        border-width: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
        border-color: Ink.accent;
        border-radius: Metric.s1;
        visible: focus.has-focus;
    }

    HorizontalLayout {
        spacing: Metric.s1;
        @children
    }

    focus := FocusScope {
        key-pressed(e) => {
            if (e.text == " " || e.text == Key.Return) {
                root.activated();
                return accept;
            }
            // §16.8: everything else bubbles. A control that swallows keys it does not use is how
            // a window ends up with three meanings for one of them.
            return reject;
        }
    }

    touch := TouchArea {
        mouse-cursor: MouseCursor.pointer;
        clicked => {
            focus.focus();
            root.activated();
        }
    }

    accessible-role: AccessibleRole.button;
    accessible-label: root.a11y-label;
    accessible-description: root.a11y-description;
    accessible-enabled: true;
    accessible-action-default => { root.activated(); }
}


// ─── The bench ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

export component Bench inherits Rectangle {
    // ── What Rust measures and pushes in ──
    //
    // `hero` is the body height in LOGICAL pixels — `k × 655.751 / sf`, where `k` is a whole number
    // decided from the measured display. It is **never** read back out of the markup and is never
    // animated, which is what keeps §6.6's arrow pointing one way.
    in property <length> hero: Geometry.hero-phys-1x;


    // ── The device ──
    //
    // `Colour::Unspecified` is `#E4E4E2` and **never black**: drawing an unknown chassis black
    // would invent a fact about somebody's iPod, and the model already refuses to (§7.2).
    in property <color> chassis: #e4e4e2;
    in property <bool> dark-chassis: false;
    // §10.1: **the drawing at `Geometry.ghost-opacity`, which is an iPod that has not been decided
    // yet.** It is an *emptiness* state and not a first-run one — §9.1 gives the later-empty bench
    // the same ghost — so it is pushed in from Rust rather than derived here from `device-name`
    // being blank or from anything else this file can see. `IPod` decides what it dims; the glass
    // is deliberately not in it.
    in property <bool> ghost: false;
    in property <image> screen-source;
    in property <bool> screen-lit: false;
    in property <string> panel-description;
    // Forwarded to the drawing; see `IPod::select-d` for why the ratio is not written in markup.
    in property <length> select-d: Geometry.centre-d * root.hero;

    // ── The machine (§7.4, §12.2) ──
    //
    // Forwarded, all of them. `has-machine` is `machine::Life::alive()` and gates the hold switch's
    // ARIA rather than the pointer stream (§7.3's announcement-not-the-gate); `hold` and the five
    // `held-*` are reads of `Stats`, so what is drawn depressed is what the machine says is down.
    in property <bool> has-machine: false;
    // §7.4's hold sentence, forwarded to the drawn device — the hold switch's ARIA description, and
    // the only thing left that reads one of these. Its twin, `wheel-refusal`, is deleted: the ring
    // has no ARIA (§16.7 concedes a 96-detent wheel has no announced equivalent) and the cradle
    // carries the drawn half, so the property had a producer and no reader — which is exactly the
    // shape this file deletes rather than keeps.
    in property <string> hold-refusal;
    in property <bool> hold: false;
    // §21.8, issue #28. See `ipod.slint`.
    in property <string> wheel-ghost;
    in property <bool> held-menu: false;
    in property <bool> held-next: false;
    in property <bool> held-prev: false;
    in property <bool> held-play: false;
    in property <bool> held-select: false;

    // ── The cradle (§7.3) ──
    //
    // The label is the one place on the bench that says **what pressing will cost**, before you
    // press — 3 seconds for a parked machine, 75 for a cold boot, four minutes for a first run,
    // instant for a title. It is the model's sentence, verbatim; this file never words it.
    in property <string> device-name;
    in property <string> cradle-label;
    /// §22.3: **there is no iPod, so the question is still open.**
    ///
    /// Pushed from `main.rs` rather than derived here, for `ghost`'s reason one property up: the
    /// window is where *what state is this program in* is decided, and a markup expression over
    /// `has-devices` would be a second answer to it. It is false the moment there is an iPod,
    /// which is §22.3's *asked once* — the question is not re-asked once it has an answer.
    in property <bool> intents: false;
    /// The first of the two. **The same act the centre button performs** — `on_start_device`, which
    /// is where the first run, a half-made device, a composed one and every refusal are told apart.
    /// A second start path here is how two of them come to disagree about whether a press may start
    /// anything, which is the note `on_verb_act` already carries about its own three rows.
    callback use-an-ipod();
    /// The second. It opens the shelf and **must not touch the device apparatus once**: `play`
    /// never calls `map_hardware`, so a person who came for a game waits for no ROM, no IPSW, no
    /// drive and no boot.
    callback play-a-game();
    in property <CradleRing> cradle-ring: CradleRing.accent;
    // §6.4, §16.6: `Path` has no dash array, so a refusal is a **broken** ring — four segments
    // with gaps at the corners. A gap is a shape and a shape is buildable, and a cradle that
    // cannot hold the device having gaps in it is the drawing saying the thing.
    in property <bool> cradle-broken: false;
    in property <bool> startable: true;
    // §12.3's boot progress. See `DeviceRow` in `primitives.slint` for why this is two properties
    // and not one, and `main::boot_rule` for the one place they are decided together.
    in property <bool> boot-measured: false;
    in property <float> boot-fraction: 0;

    // ── §9.5's pane ──
    //
    // **Computed in Rust with hysteresis and pushed in**, because Slint has no responsive
    // breakpoints and cannot have any (`window.slint`'s own note). True below the threshold; the
    // well and everything in it stops being drawn and `ShortPane` stands in its place.
    in property <bool> too-short: false;
    in property <string> short-headline;
    in property <string> short-body;
    // §7.3's caption again, worded for a surface that draws no centre button. The same property
    // the cradle label is fed from, through `main::Press` — one tail, two prefixes.
    in property <string> press-label;

    // ── The caption, above and below the device ──
    //
    // §7.5's three shelf rows, each now beside the thing it describes. **The two the shelf had
    // left are gone rather than relocated**: the device's parts are `About this iPod`'s six facts
    // on the drawer's root page (§21.3), and `k` is §12.9's own argument against itself — §15
    // struck the other three numbers out of that slot for being instrumentation on a band with no
    // exit, and the same reasoning finishes the job. A fact drawn twice on one screen is one fact
    // too many.
    in property <string> caption-name;
    in property <string> caption-state;
    // §11.4's row 2, and its only tenant. Empty at rest.
    in property <string> drop-what;
    // §7.6: this device cannot start, so the cradle label is `blocked_label`'s sentence and the
    // `why ›` beside it opens the drawer at the page owning the paragraph.
    in property <bool> refused: false;

    // ── `:focus-visible`, hand-built, because Slint has no such thing ──
    //
    // **The cradle's focus ring is drawn when focus arrived by KEYBOARD, and not when it arrived
    // by pointer.** The operator's report is what this is for: *"i also dont like the border
    // around the ipod and the additional selected border when i press on the ipod."* The second
    // half is exactly this ring — pressing the device runs `cradle-focus.focus()` (§7.2: a click
    // on the body IS focusing the fixture), so a person driving with a mouse got an indicator
    // meant for somebody driving with `Tab`.
    //
    // **Deleting it was not an option** and §16.7 and §14.1 both say why: it is how a keyboard
    // user knows where they are, and `the_centre_button_is_reachable_from_the_keyboard_with_no_
    // pointer` is a test this program already passes. So this is the web's `:focus-visible` rule
    // rather than a hidden ring — the *last input modality* decides, not the focus event:
    //
    //   * a pointer press on the drawn device sets it **false**, at the four press edges below —
    //     `clicked`, `pressed-device`, `wheel-down`, `centre-down` and `hold-pressed`, which are
    //     the pointer's own callbacks. `centre-down` is also raised from `cradle-focus`'s
    //     `key-pressed`; that site is a different one and does not clear it, which is why this is
    //     written at the forwarding sites rather than on the callbacks themselves;
    //   * `Tab` sets it **true**, through `focus-gained`'s `FocusReason` — Slint gives the reason
    //     and this is the one place it is worth having;
    //   * **any key** at the cradle sets it **true**, which is the recovery a rule keyed on the
    //     focus event alone would not have: click the device, then reach for the keyboard, and the
    //     ring is back before the first keystroke does anything.
    //
    // **`Programmatic` and `WindowActivation` deliberately leave it alone.** `window.slint`'s
    // `init` hands the cradle focus with `focus-cradle()`, and so does closing the drawer with
    // `⌘\` — carrying the last modality forward through those is the whole of what makes a
    // keyboard session keep its ring and a pointer session keep its clean device. It starts
    // **true**, because a window nobody has touched yet has had no pointer input to hide it for.
    //
    // `out` rather than `private`, so `window.slint` can mirror it to Rust for
    // `the_focus_ring_follows_the_modality_focus_arrived_by`. Nothing outside this component
    // writes it.
    out property <bool> focus-visible: true;
    // The Rail's current line, at bench scale. `Rail::line()` is where the precedence is decided —
    // a failure outranks work in progress — and this is its one use site. Empty means the Rail has
    // nothing to say and the write target stands.
    in property <string> rail-line;
    in property <string> write-target;
    // §7.5: `warn` only on `writes to <your>.img` — the case where nobody said it was a copy and
    // we did not build the image either.
    in property <bool> write-warn: false;
    in property <bool> drawer-open: false;
    // §12.3, and the sign is load-bearing: **negative means there is no denominator**, so there is
    // no bar — a number that moves instead. A bar with an invented denominator is a lying
    // instrument, and a boot has no honest one until this device has completed one.
    in property <float> progress: -1;

    callback pressed-centre();
    // §7.4's pointer stream, straight through. In units of the wheel's outer radius — see
    // `IPod::wheel-down`, which is where the normalisation and the reason for it live.
    callback wheel-down(float, float);
    callback wheel-moved(float, float);
    callback wheel-up();
    // §16.11's scroll over the ring, in logical pixels of `delta-y` — the one member of this group
    // that is NOT normalised, because it is measured against the pointing device rather than
    // against the drawing. See `IPod::wheel-scrolled`.
    callback wheel-scrolled(float);
    callback centre-down();
    callback centre-up();
    callback hold-pressed();
    callback hold-released();
    callback open-drawer();
    callback why();

    // §7.3: **the cradle is the Button**, so it is what a window hands focus to when it opens. The
    // fixture is an internal element, so the route to it has to be a function on the component —
    // `forward-focus` cannot reach inside one.
    //
    // **And below §9.5's threshold the cradle is not drawn**, so the same call has to land on the
    // pane's Row instead: that Row is the press on that surface, and a window that opened on a
    // 1280 x 800 display with focus on an invisible fixture would have `Return` doing nothing on
    // the one control the whole program is built around. It routes on the state rather than being
    // two functions, so a caller cannot pick the wrong one.
    //
    // **It does not chase the threshold**, and that is deliberate rather than missing: nothing here
    // moves focus when the window is dragged across it, because focus may well be on a drawer row
    // by then and stealing it mid-drag is worse than leaving it. `Tab` reaches the pane's Row, and
    // `visible` takes the undrawn half out of the tab order (§16.3).
    // **The keyboard was used somewhere this component cannot see.** §16.8 sends `↑ ↓ ← →` to the
    // window's ROOT `FocusScope` rather than to the cradle's — they are the wheel, and every scope
    // below returns `reject` for them — so the one thing a keyboard user does most on this surface
    // never reaches `cradle-focus.key-pressed`. `window.slint` calls this instead. See
    // `focus-visible` for what it is for.
    public function keyboard-was-used() {
        root.focus-visible = true;
    }

    public function focus-cradle() {
        if (root.too-short) {
            short.take-focus();
        } else {
            cradle-focus.focus();
        }
    }

    // ── The device's place, and the whole of §7.2 in three lines ──
    //
    // **Pinned by its distance above the bottom edge, not centred in the well.** All vertical
    // slack goes to the top margin, so growing the window moves nothing, shrinking it eats the top
    // margin first, and anything that pushes from the top costs the well its air rather than the
    // device its place.
    //
    // The column below the body, from §9.6, bottom up: `GAP_2`, the cradle label, `GAP_1`, and
    // `CRADLE_BAND` — the overhang plus the focus ring, which is the term the previous revision
    // listed once and lost off the top edge. §7.5's shelf was the bottom term and is gone.
    property <length> body-w: Geometry.body-aspect * root.hero;
    property <length> body-x: (well.width - root.body-w) / 2;
    property <length> body-y: well.height - Geometry.gap-2 - Geometry.cradle-label
        - Geometry.gap-1 - Geometry.cradle-band - root.hero;

    // The cradle's corner follows the body's, offset outward by the same 10 px the outline is:
    // 0.0501 × 655.751 + 10 = 42.8 at hero, which is §7.3's number and is derived rather than
    // typed so a re-measured `CORNER_R` moves it.
    property <length> cradle-r: Geometry.corner-r * root.hero + Geometry.cradle-overhang;

    // §6.4, and **the numbers are computed rather than eyeballed.** An earlier draft drew the
    // inactive ring as `line` at 30 %, which is **1.23 : 1** on `bg-sunken` — the only surface a
    // cradle is ever drawn on — across five of its twelve states, i.e. invisible.
    // `the_cradle_colours_clear_three_to_one_against_the_well` reads this very statement, pulls
    // the roles out of it, and recomputes all three against the well.
    property <color> ring-ink: root.cradle-ring == CradleRing.danger ? Ink.danger
        : root.cradle-ring == CradleRing.accent ? Ink.accent
        : Ink.fg-dim;

    // §7.4: pressing an inert drawn control puts its sentence on the **cradle label**, held while
    // the pointer is down and released on pointer-up. Not a four-second message under the device:
    // that is a self-dismissing timed message replacing content, which is a toast, which principle
    // 5 bans by name — and it would have let a refusal like *the disk is not where it was* be
    // silently overwritten because somebody brushed the wheel.
    //
    // **Pushed, where it used to be assigned here from `IPod`'s `inert` callback.** Two things made
    // that untenable at once: the sentence has to say which of §7.4's two states the bench is in,
    // which is a fact only `machine::Life` holds; and §16.8 gives the same controls keys, which
    // arrive at the window's root `FocusScope` and never come near this component. One writer, in
    // Rust, so a refused key and a refused press cannot end up saying different things.
    in property <string> held-sentence;

    // The bench reports no intrinsic height of its own. Without this the well's content would be
    // deciding the height the well is measured against — the same loop as a device that resizes
    // itself, one level down (§16.1).
    min-height: 0px;
    preferred-height: 0px;

    background: Ink.bg-sunken;
    accessible-role: AccessibleRole.main;
    // **The one place *bench* was a word a person met**, and §21.2 retires that framing: the
    // window is the device, so the main region an assistive technology announces is the iPod
    // rather than a workbench holding machines you have parked.
    accessible-label: "the iPod";

    // ── §7.1 The well ──
    //
    // Full bleed, `bg-sunken`. It is a recess in both schemes, which is the rule that generates the
    // dark value rather than a taste. It holds one device and nothing else, ever.
    // ── The way in (§4) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
    //
    well := Rectangle {
        x: 0;
        y: 0;
        width: parent.width;
        height: parent.height;
        // See the header: this is not §9.5's pane, and it is not to be mistaken for one. What it
        // buys is that a caption laid out past the bottom edge is clipped rather than drawn over
        // the edge of the client — during a drag that has not yet crossed the threshold, and
        // above it at every size.
        clip: true;
        // §9.5, and **`visible` rather than `if`** (§16.3), for two reasons that both bite. The
        // subtree holds `cradle-focus` and the drawn device's own hover and press state, and `if`
        // destroys all of it on every crossing of the threshold — the carousel defect, once per
        // drag. And `body-x` / `body-y` above read `well.width` and `well.height`, which an `if`
        // element cannot be asked for by name. What `visible: false` costs is nothing here: this
        // element is absolutely positioned, so there is no layout cell to be reserved, and Slint
        // takes an invisible subtree out of the tab order and out of the accessible tree — which is
        // the same mechanism `drawer.slint` uses to stop a closed drawer being announced.
        visible: !root.too-short;

        // The frame holds the device and its cradle together, so the one animated property on this
        // surface moves both and they cannot come apart. §8.1 item 1: **only `x` animates.**
        // ── What is on the bench, above it (§7.5 row 1, relocated) ──────────────────────────
        //
        // **§7.5's first row said this, 88 px of chrome away from the thing it describes.**
        // The name of the machine and what it is doing belong beside the machine: a person
        // looking at a booting iPod should not have to look at the bottom of the window to find
        // out that it is booting.
        //
        // Drawn only when there is something to say. `caption-name` is empty on an empty bench, and
        // §9.1 forbids a bare *nothing here* — the well's own empty state is the sentence there.
        //
        // **Two lines, and the second one is reserved rather than conditional.** §11.4's band is a
        // count, a *let go* and at most two identifications, and the identifications had nowhere
        // to go when the shelf did: they are the whole of *what the program thinks it has been
        // handed*, so deleting the bar without giving them a home would have deleted the answer.
        // The slot is held open at every size because §11.4's own rule is that **a band that
        // reflows is a band that moves**, on the one surface drawn while somebody is holding a
        // mouse button down — and a line that appeared only during a drag would move the name of
        // the machine every time a file crossed the window edge. It costs `LINE_BODY`, against the
        // 44 px `SHELF` the column just gave back, so the device is 24 px better off.
        //
        // **Named, so one test can address it.** `the_caption_above_the_device_sums_to_its_rows`
        // is U-1 pointed at what replaced the shelf: the declared height, the two line boxes
        // inside it and the `y` that stands it on the cradle band all have to agree, or a row is
        // drawn outside the block that reserved the column height for it.
        caption := VerticalLayout {
            x: root.body-x;
            y: root.body-y - Geometry.cradle-band - Geometry.line-title - Geometry.line-body;
            width: root.body-w;
            height: Geometry.line-title + Geometry.line-body;
            spacing: 0;
            visible: root.caption-name != "";

            HorizontalLayout {
                height: Geometry.line-title;
                spacing: Metric.s2;

                Text {
                    text: root.caption-name;
                    font-size: Metric.title-size;
                    font-weight: Metric.weight-title;
                    color: Ink.fg;
                    vertical-alignment: center;
                    overflow: elide;
                    horizontal-stretch: 1;
                }

                Text {
                    text: root.caption-state;
                    font-size: Metric.label-size;
                    font-weight: Metric.weight-label;
                    color: Ink.fg-dim;
                    vertical-alignment: center;
                    overflow: elide;
                }
            }

            // §11.4's row 2. Empty except while a drag is over the window — `main.rs` pushes the
            // identifications and nothing else ever writes here, so the reserved line is blank at
            // rest rather than carrying a second copy of a fact drawn somewhere else. That is
            // §21.3's own demotion applied to the last thing the shelf held: the device's parts
            // are `About this iPod`'s six facts on the drawer's root page, and they are not
            // repeated on the bench.
            Text {
                height: Geometry.line-body;
                text: root.drop-what;
                font-size: Metric.body-size;
                font-weight: Metric.weight-body;
                color: Ink.fg-dim;
                vertical-alignment: center;
                overflow: elide;
            }
        }

        frame := Rectangle {
            x: root.body-x - Geometry.cradle-band;
            y: root.body-y - Geometry.cradle-band;
            width: root.body-w + 2 * Geometry.cradle-band;
            height: root.hero + 2 * Geometry.cradle-band;
            // **No `animate x` here, and there is now nothing left for one to chase.** §8.1 item 1
            // spends its ONE `gentle` on the drawer's own `x`, and §21.2 stopped that `x` from
            // reaching this one: `well.width` is `parent.width`, `body-x` is derived from it, and
            // opening the drawer moves neither. The device is at the same place open or shut,
            // which is the whole of *the drawer overlays instead of pushing*.

            // §6.4: the cradle's focus ring is `fg`, and it is the one exception to *the focus
            // ring is `accent`*. Everywhere else the ring sits on `bg` or `bg-raised` where the
            // accent clears 5 : 1; here a 2 px accent ring 4 px outside a 2 px accent state ring
            // is a ring around a ring in one colour, which is not a focus indicator. `fg` is
            // 13.3 : 1 on the well and collides with no state.
            //
            // Instant, never animated (§8.1 item 9).
            //
            // **The halo, and it is why the ring stopped reading as a box round the product**
            // (§23, 2026-09-07). `Ink.fg` is `#000000` — the hardest ink this palette has — and a
            // 2 px pure-black outline is what the operator was looking at when he said *"i dont
            // like the border around the ipod"*. §6.4's arithmetic for choosing it is untouched and
            // still right: the well is `bg-sunken`, the accent is already the state ring's, and
            // `fg` at 13.3 : 1 is the one role that collides with nothing.
            //
            // What was missing was not contrast but **falloff**. A hard stroke with nothing outside
            // it is a drawn rectangle; the same stroke with a one-pixel accent-tinted band around
            // it is an indicator sitting on a surface. It is the construction `Pressable` now uses
            // for every row in the drawer, so the window has one focus ring rather than two, and it
            // costs the cradle no state and no colour it did not already have.
            //
            // Declared first so the crisp ring paints over its inner edge, and carrying the same
            // `visible` — the modality gate is the ring's whole point (§16.7) and a halo that
            // outlived it would be the permanent box again, one pixel out.
            Rectangle {
                x: 0 - Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                y: 0 - Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                width: parent.width + 2 * Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                height: parent.height + 2 * Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                border-width: Geometry.focus-ring-w + Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                border-color: Ink.focus-halo;
                border-radius: root.cradle-r + Geometry.focus-gap + Geometry.focus-halo-w;
                visible: cradle-focus.has-focus && root.focus-visible;
            }

            Rectangle {
                x: 0;
                y: 0;
                width: 100%;
                height: 100%;
                border-width: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                border-color: Ink.fg;
                border-radius: root.cradle-r + Geometry.focus-gap;
                // **Both halves, and the second one is the whole of the fix.** `has-focus` alone
                // drew this whenever the cradle held focus, which is most of a session — the
                // window hands it the cradle at `init` and a click on the device gives it back —
                // so a transient indicator was a permanent box around the product. See
                // `focus-visible` above for why the answer is a modality rather than a timer.
                visible: cradle-focus.has-focus && root.focus-visible;
            }

            // The whole ring — one outline, 2 px, tracing the body's footprint 10 px out.
            Rectangle {
                x: Geometry.focus-gap;
                y: Geometry.focus-gap;
                width: parent.width - 2 * Geometry.focus-gap;
                height: parent.height - 2 * Geometry.focus-gap;
                border-width: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                border-color: root.ring-ink;
                border-radius: root.cradle-r;
                visible: !root.cradle-broken;
                // §8.1 item 5: colour and ring continuity only, `tight`. Its geometry never moves.
                animate border-color { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // …and the broken one: the same outline with its four corners missing. `visible`
            // rather than `if`, per §16.3 — both are absolutely positioned, so nothing moves
            // either way, and `if` would destroy and rebuild the subtree on a state change.
            broken := Rectangle {
                x: Geometry.focus-gap;
                y: Geometry.focus-gap;
                width: parent.width - 2 * Geometry.focus-gap;
                height: parent.height - 2 * Geometry.focus-gap;
                visible: root.cradle-broken;

                Rectangle {
                    x: root.cradle-r;
                    y: 0;
                    width: parent.width - 2 * root.cradle-r;
                    height: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    background: root.ring-ink;
                }
                Rectangle {
                    x: root.cradle-r;
                    y: parent.height - Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    width: parent.width - 2 * root.cradle-r;
                    height: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    background: root.ring-ink;
                }
                Rectangle {
                    x: 0;
                    y: root.cradle-r;
                    width: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    height: parent.height - 2 * root.cradle-r;
                    background: root.ring-ink;
                }
                Rectangle {
                    x: parent.width - Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    y: root.cradle-r;
                    width: Geometry.focus-ring-w;
                    height: parent.height - 2 * root.cradle-r;
                    background: root.ring-ink;
                }
            }

            // The two clamp marks, at the body's mid-height on either side. Centred **on** the
            // ring's own line, so the fixture reads as gripping the device rather than as two
            // ticks near it.
            Rectangle {
                x: Geometry.focus-gap + (Geometry.focus-ring-w - Geometry.clamp-w) / 2;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: Geometry.clamp-w;
                height: Geometry.clamp-h;
                background: root.ring-ink;
                border-radius: 1px;
                animate background { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }
            Rectangle {
                x: parent.width - Geometry.focus-gap - (Geometry.focus-ring-w + Geometry.clamp-w) / 2;
                y: (parent.height - self.height) / 2;
                width: Geometry.clamp-w;
                height: Geometry.clamp-h;
                background: root.ring-ink;
                border-radius: 1px;
                animate background { duration: Motion.tight; easing: ease-out-quad; }
            }

            // ── §12.3's boot rule ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
            //
            // **Drawn only when there is a denominator**, which is the whole of §12.3: *"before a
            // device has ever booted there is no fraction and no bar."* `boot-measured` is false
            // for a device whose last cold boot was never observed, and then this element is not
            // there at all — the caption carries `booting — 412 M instr`, a number that moves,
            // which is what that paragraph asks for instead.
            //
            // **In the cradle's own band, under the body**, so it costs nothing in §9.6's column
            // and sits on the fixture rather than on the object (principle 3).
            //
            // **No `animate`.** The fraction is republished up to sixty times a second by the
            // machine tick, so an animation would be easing toward a target that has already
            // moved — the same argument the cradle label two elements down makes about its own
            // `x`, and §8.2's rule with teeth.
            //
            // It carries no accessible role deliberately: this is the *picture* of a number the
            // cradle label is already saying in words, and a second announcement of one fact is
            // §16.7's noise rather than its access.
            Rectangle {
                x: Geometry.cradle-band;
                y: Geometry.cradle-band + root.hero + Geometry.boot-rule-gap;
                width: root.body-w;
                height: Geometry.boot-rule-h;
                visible: root.boot-measured;
                background: Ink.line;
                border-radius: self.height / 2;

                Rectangle {
                    x: 0;
                    y: 0;
                    // **Already 0..1 when it arrives, and the clamp is `main::boot_rule`'s.**
                    // `machine::Progress::percent` is deliberately unclamped — a reading over
                    // 100 % means the recipe changed under a stored denominator and §12.3 wants
                    // that visible in the caption — but a rule cannot be longer than its track, so
                    // the drawing gets a clamped fraction and the words keep the true one. Written
                    // as `clamp(root.boot-fraction, 0, 1)` here first, which
                    // `no_geometry_literal_survives_in_the_markup` refused: a bound in an
                    // expression is still a number in the markup, and the sweep was right that it
                    // belongs on the other side of the boundary.
                    width: parent.width * root.boot-fraction;
                    height: parent.height;
                    background: Ink.accent;
                    border-radius: parent.border-radius;
                }
            }

            // §7.3: **the cradle is the `Button`.** The pointer route to starting is the drawn
            // centre button, which is the device's own; this is the keyboard and screen-reader
            // route to the same callback, so the two cannot disagree about what pressing means.
            //
            // Declared **before** the drawing so the drawing's own touch areas sit on top of it: a
            // `FocusScope` accepts a pointer press to take focus, and one declared later would
            // swallow every press on the device. What is left underneath is the 16 px band of
            // fixture around the body, where clicking focuses the cradle and does nothing else —
            // which is what a fixture should do.
            cradle-focus := FocusScope {
                x: 0;
                y: 0;
                width: 100%;
                height: 100%;
                // **Ungated, exactly like the pointer route** (§7.4, §16.8). The drawn centre button
                // calls `pressed-centre` whatever the state of the device, because Rust is the only
                // thing that can say *which part is gone* and a press that teaches nothing is worse
                // than a press that is refused. A `if (root.startable)` here made `Return` a dead key
                // on the one device §20 item 12 exists for, while a click on the same device filed
                // the refusal — the two disagreeing about what pressing means, in the file whose
                // comment says they cannot.
                //
                // **And it raised only ONE of the pointer's two callbacks, which made it a dead key
                // again** — this time over a *running* machine, and for two years of the design
                // saying otherwise. §16.8's table promises `Enter` / `Space` are *the primary
                // action — on the bench, the centre button*; a click on that button raises
                // `centre-down` / `centre-up`, which is the half `machine::centre` answers
                // `ToMachine` for, and `pressed-centre`, which is the half that starts or stops
                // one. This raised the second only, and `on_start_device`'s `ToMachine` arm
                // returns without sending anything — by design, because the drawn button under it
                // was supposed to be doing the other half. So over a running iPod, `Enter` and
                // `Space` queued **nothing at all**: measured, on a wired bench with a machine on
                // it, `[]` for both. Both edges are raised now, in the pointer's own order.
                //
                // **`held` is §16.8's `down_keys` in one boolean, and it is not optional.** Slint's
                // winit backend maps an auto-repeat to a plain `KeyPressed` — it reads
                // `event.state` and drops winit's own `repeat` flag
                // (`i-slint-backend-winit-1.17.1/event_loop.rs:340-344`) — so a held `Enter` is a
                // stream of presses with no releases between them. Without the guard, holding it
                // over an idle device starts the machine on the first repeat and sends
                // `Cmd::PowerOff` on the second, because by then §7.3's `booting` row makes the
                // same control *press ● to stop*. One keystroke is one press, and the release is
                // honoured only for a press this scope took — which is the same rule, and the same
                // sentence, as the root scope's.
                property <bool> held;
                // **`Tab` arrived here, so the ring is wanted.** Slint's own focus system moves on
                // `Tab` and reports why through `FocusReason`, which is the one place this program
                // needs it: `tab-navigation` is a keyboard user landing on the cradle and is the
                // case §16.7 exists for. `programmatic` and `window-activation` are left alone on
                // purpose — `window.slint`'s `init` and `⌘\` both use the first, and neither is a
                // statement about which device somebody is driving with.
                focus-gained(reason) => {
                    if (reason == FocusReason.tab-navigation) {
                        root.focus-visible = true;
                    }
                }
                key-pressed(e) => {
                    // **Any key, not only the two below**, and it is the half a rule keyed on the
                    // focus event alone would miss: after a click the ring is off, and a person who
                    // then reaches for the keyboard should see where they are BEFORE the keystroke
                    // does anything rather than after. Arrow keys reach the root scope rather than
                    // this one, so they set it there.
                    root.focus-visible = true;
                    if (e.text == " " || e.text == Key.Return) {
                        if (!self.held) {
                            self.held = true;
                            root.centre-down();
                            root.pressed-centre();
                        }
                        return accept;
                    }
                    return reject;
                }
                key-released(e) => {
                    if (e.text == " " || e.text == Key.Return) {
                        if (self.held) {
                            self.held = false;
                            root.centre-up();
                        }
                        return accept;
                    }
                    return reject;
                }
                // **And the hole a boolean keyed on focus has that the root scope's `down_keys`
                // does not: focus can leave while the key is still down.** `Tab` is not swallowed
                // by the two arms above, so holding `Enter` and pressing `Tab` moves focus, the
                // release goes to whoever has it now, and this scope's `held` would stay true with
                // `Button(SELECT, true)` standing on the machine — the stuck finger, reached from
                // the one direction the guard itself opens. Losing focus ends the press, which is
                // also what a real thumb leaving a real button does.
                focus-lost => {
                    if (self.held) {
                        self.held = false;
                        root.centre-up();
                    }
                }
                accessible-role: AccessibleRole.button;
                accessible-label: root.device-name;
                // **The same expression the label below draws, and it was `cradle-label` alone.**
                // §7.4's refusal is drawn on that row and is the only thing a drawn control says
                // when it is pressed with no machine behind it — so an assistive technology heard
                // *press ● to cold boot* while the screen read *the wheel and the buttons belong to
                // the machine…*. §16.7's rule is that an ARIA claim must be true as built, and two
                // strings for one row is that rule broken by the one sentence §16.7 already
                // concedes the ring has no announced equivalent for.
                accessible-description: root.held-sentence != "" ? root.held-sentence : root.cradle-label;
                // The announcement, not the gate: an assistive technology is told this device cannot
                // be started, and pressing it still produces the sentence saying why.
                accessible-enabled: root.startable;
                // **All three, in the pointer's order**, for the reason the key handler above
                // gives: a default action that raised `pressed-centre` alone did nothing whatever
                // over a running machine, so an assistive technology was announcing a button that
                // could not press Select. There is no duration to carry here and none is needed —
                // `emu::schedule_at` pushes a button's release out to `MIN_BUTTON_HOLD_USEC`
                // whatever the window says, so a synthesised tap is held long enough to be read.
                accessible-action-default => {
                    root.centre-down();
                    root.centre-up();
                    root.pressed-centre();
                }
            }

            device := IPod {
                x: Geometry.cradle-band;
                y: Geometry.cradle-band;
                body-height: root.hero;
                chassis: root.chassis;
                dark-chassis: root.dark-chassis;
                ghost: root.ghost;
                screen-source: root.screen-source;
                screen-lit: root.screen-lit;
                panel-description: root.panel-description;
                select-d: root.select-d;
                pressable: true;
                has-machine: root.has-machine;
                hold-refusal: root.hold-refusal;
                hold: root.hold;
                wheel-ghost: root.wheel-ghost;
                held-menu: root.held-menu;
                held-next: root.held-next;
                held-prev: root.held-prev;
                held-play: root.held-play;
                held-select: root.held-select;

                // **Every press edge below clears `focus-visible` first.** These five are the
                // pointer's own callbacks and nothing else raises them, which is what makes this
                // the honest place to record the modality: `centre-down` is raised from
                // `cradle-focus.key-pressed` as well, and clearing it there would hide the ring
                // from the keyboard user it exists for.
                pressed-device => { root.focus-visible = false; root.pressed-centre(); }
                // §7.2 deletes device-switching from the bench — while there is a machine the bench
                // draws it and only it, and other devices are inspected on the Devices drawer page
                // — so a press on the body is not *select this one* any more; there is nothing else
                // to select. It focuses the fixture, which is the thing §7.3 makes the Button.
                // **A callback for the old meaning is not kept**: a callback nobody wires is the
                // shape §16.9 deletes.
                //
                // The modality is cleared **before** `.focus()`, and the order does not actually
                // matter — the focus that follows is `FocusReason.programmatic`, which
                // `focus-gained` deliberately passes over — but writing it first says what the
                // press meant rather than relying on that.
                clicked => { root.focus-visible = false; cradle-focus.focus(); }
                wheel-down(dx, dy) => { root.focus-visible = false; root.wheel-down(dx, dy); }
                wheel-moved(dx, dy) => { root.wheel-moved(dx, dy); }
                wheel-up => { root.wheel-up(); }
                wheel-scrolled(dy) => { root.wheel-scrolled(dy); }
                centre-down => { root.focus-visible = false; root.centre-down(); }
                centre-up => { root.centre-up(); }
                hold-pressed => { root.focus-visible = false; root.hold-pressed(); }
                hold-released => { root.hold-released(); }
            }
        }

        // ── The cradle label, and §7.6's `why ›` beside it ──
        //
        // 24 px, `body` 14 / 20, centred, under the fixture. It is the cradle's, not the device's,
        // which is why it moves with the frame's x rather than with the well's centre.
        //
        // **`why ›` came here from the shelf and it came to the right place.** §7.6 called it a
        // compromise in as many words: the reason belongs next to the control (principle 4), and
        // `Verdict::No.why` runs two to four sentences, which did not fit a shelf row — so the
        // shelf elided a summary and offered a route to the paragraph. This line is where the
        // refusal is now WORDED — `machine::cradle` returns `blocked_label`'s sentence for a
        // device that cannot start — so the route out of it is one slot away from the thing it
        // explains rather than 88 px of chrome below it.
        //
        // **`if`, not `visible`** (§16.3): the cell is ~35 px and this row is one elided line at a
        // measure the device's own width sets. §16.3's anti-shuffle argument does not reach it —
        // the label is `horizontal-stretch: 1` and takes whatever is left, so nothing below moves
        // when the control appears. What moves is the label's own centring, and a refusal arriving
        // is a state change the reader has to notice.
        HorizontalLayout {
            x: frame.x;
            y: frame.y + frame.height + Geometry.gap-1;
            width: frame.width;
            height: Geometry.cradle-label;
            spacing: Metric.s2;
            // **No `animate x` here.** `frame.x` is already eased, so a second animation on this
            // binding would chase an already-moving target and the label would trail the device it
            // belongs to. One animated property on this surface, and this reads it.

            // ── §22.3's two intents, on the one screen where the question is live ────────────
            //
            // **A person arriving wants one of two things**, and until now this line offered only
            // one of them: *Press the centre button to make an iPod*. Somebody who came to play a
            // game was told to build an iPod first — and `play` never calls `map_hardware`, so
            // that is not merely a longer route, it is a route to somewhere they did not need to
            // go. No ROM, no IPSW, no drive, no boot: a title is bare RAM, the image, and the
            // sixty stubs its imports trap into.
            //
            // **On the drawn iPod rather than in front of it, and that is §22.7's own line.**
            // §22.3 sketches this as a chooser standing where the device is; §22.7's list of what
            // §22 does *not* overturn opens with §21.2's *the window is the iPod*. Those two cannot
            // both be had, and the picture settles it — `_out/gui/bench-empty.png` is the one page
            // in this program that looks like the thing the program is, and putting two rectangles
            // in front of it would answer *complicated and ugly* with a form. So the choice is
            // offered **on** the device, on the line a person is already reading, in the slot §7.6
            // built for exactly this shape of control.
            //
            // **`if`, not `visible`**, for the identical reason `why ›` gives one element down: the
            // label is `horizontal-stretch: 1` and takes whatever is left, so nothing below moves
            // when these appear or go.
            //
            // **Both are drawn only where `intents` is true**, which `main.rs` pushes as *there is
            // no iPod and the drawer is shut* — §22.3's *asked once*. With an iPod on the bench the
            // question is answered and the line goes back to saying what a press costs.
            // **The two, in one stretched cell, so they centre together.** A pair of controls at
            // either end of a hidden spring is two choices at opposite edges of the device; the
            // question is one question and reads as one only if the answers are adjacent.
            if root.intents: HorizontalLayout {
                horizontal-stretch: 1;
                alignment: center;
                spacing: Metric.s5;

                BenchControl {
                    a11y-label: "Use an iPod";
                    // The bill and the route, which is what the line said before these two arrived
                    // — *Press the centre button to make an iPod*. It is the same act, so it is the
                    // same sentence, fetched rather than reworded.
                    a11y-description: root.cradle-label;
                    activated => { root.use-an-ipod(); }

                    Text {
                        text: "Use an iPod";
                        font-size: Metric.body-size;
                        font-weight: Metric.weight-strong;
                        color: Ink.accent;
                        vertical-alignment: center;
                    }
                    Chevron { ink: Ink.accent; }
                }

                BenchControl {
                    a11y-label: "Play a game";
                    a11y-description: "opens the shelf of titles. A game needs no iPod at all";
                    activated => { root.play-a-game(); }

                    Text {
                        text: "Play a game";
                        font-size: Metric.body-size;
                        font-weight: Metric.weight-strong;
                        color: Ink.accent;
                        vertical-alignment: center;
                    }
                    Chevron { ink: Ink.accent; }
                }
            }

            // **`if`, not `visible`, and this is the one place on this line where that is the
            // right way round.** §16.3's rule reserves a cell wherever something below would
            // otherwise shuffle; here the row's height is pinned at `Geometry.cradle-label` and
            // these two are alternatives filling one line, so nothing can move either way. What
            // `visible: false` *does* cost is the cell itself: a hidden `Text` still reports its
            // preferred width, and this sentence is long — so with it in the layout the two
            // controls above were shoved to the leading edge instead of centring. Read it off
            // `_out/gui/bench-empty.png` before this change: `Use an iPod › Play a game ›` hard
            // against the left of the device.
            //
            // `press-label` still says *Press the centre button to make an iPod* and the centre
            // button still does it — `Use an iPod` is that same act, named — so drawing both would
            // be one route stated twice on one line in 420 px. The sentence is not lost: it is the
            // control's own `accessible-description`.
            if !root.intents: Text {
                text: root.held-sentence != "" ? root.held-sentence : root.cradle-label;
                font-size: Metric.body-size;
                font-weight: Metric.weight-body;
                color: Ink.fg-dim;
                horizontal-alignment: center;
                vertical-alignment: center;
                overflow: elide;
                horizontal-stretch: 1;
            }

            if root.refused: BenchControl {
                a11y-label: "why";
                a11y-description: "opens the drawer at the page that owns this refusal";
                activated => { root.why(); }

                Text {
                    text: "why";
                    font-size: Metric.label-size;
                    font-weight: Metric.weight-label;
                    color: Ink.accent;
                    vertical-alignment: center;
                }
                Chevron { ink: Ink.accent; }
            }
        }

        // ── What just happened, and what it is writing to (§7.5 row 2 and row 3, relocated) ──
        //
        // Two facts under one line, and the priority is which one is NEWS. The Rail's sentence is
        // transient — *titles — 20 titles*, *the settings could not be written* — and while there
        // is one it is what a person is waiting to read. The write target is permanent and is the
        // more important of the two: it is the line standing between an afternoon and somebody's
        // only image of an iPod they own, so it is what this says whenever there is no news.
        //
        // **This is the one row of §7.5's shelf that could not simply be deleted.** The other
        // four are drawn elsewhere or are diagnostics; `write_target` is drawn nowhere else in
        // the program, and deleting the bar without giving it a home would have removed a safety
        // sentence rather than a piece of chrome.
        Text {
            x: frame.x;
            y: frame.y + frame.height + Geometry.gap-1 + Geometry.cradle-label;
            width: frame.width;
            height: Geometry.line-label;
            text: root.rail-line != "" ? root.rail-line : root.write-target;
            font-size: Metric.label-size;
            font-weight: Metric.weight-label;
            color: root.write-warn && root.rail-line == "" ? Ink.warn : Ink.fg-dim;
            horizontal-alignment: center;
            vertical-alignment: center;
            overflow: elide;
        }
    }

    // ── §9.5 The replacement pane ──
    //
    // **The well's own box, exactly** — `well.width` and `well.height` rather than the same two
    // expressions written a second time, so the pane cannot come to stand somewhere the device was
    // not. `visible` is the other half of the well's, and the two are the same boolean read twice
    // rather than a state machine: exactly one of them is drawn at every size.
    short := ShortPane {
        x: 0;
        y: 0;
        width: well.width;
        height: well.height;
        visible: root.too-short;

        headline: root.short-headline;
        body: root.short-body;
        // §9.2 again: while something is happening the caption carries it, and this Row is the
        // caption on this surface — so it reads the same two properties the cradle label does,
        // in the same order.
        press-label: root.press-label;
        startable: root.startable;

        // **The cradle's own callback, and there is no second route.** §9.5: *one source, so they
        // cannot disagree.* Rust resolves the device and either starts it or files the refusal.
        pressed => {
            root.pressed-centre();
        }
    }

    // ── §9.2's one line of work, and it is all that is left of the shelf ──
    //
    // §7.5's band held three rows and ended holding one: the device's parts and `k`. Both are now
    // somewhere better — the parts are `About this iPod`'s six facts on the drawer's root page
    // (§21.3, *demoted, not deleted*), and `k` is §12.9's own argument against itself, which §15
    // already applied to the other three numbers that slot carried. So the band goes, and with it
    // its gradient, its top rule, its `content-info` landmark and the 44 px the column was giving
    // up to draw a second copy of what the drawer says.
    //
    // **The bar is not chrome and does not go.** It is the only drawn indication that a fetch or a
    // build is running — the cradle label says so in words, and §9.2 asks the bench to mirror the
    // Rail — and it cost no height on the shelf's top rule for exactly the reason it costs none
    // here: 3 px painted OVER the bottom of the well, not a band under it. Nothing is laid out
    // against it, so the device does not move when work starts.
    //
    // `linear` and never eased — it is data, and a bar that overshoots is lying (§8's table). No
    // `animate` at all: the value moves when data moves.
    Rectangle {
        x: 0;
        y: parent.height - Geometry.progress-h;
        width: parent.width * root.progress;
        height: Geometry.progress-h;
        background: Ink.accent;
        visible: root.progress >= 0;
        accessible-role: AccessibleRole.progress-indicator;
        accessible-label: "progress";
        // **The Rail's line, not the device's facts.** It read the device summary — which is not
        // what this bar is about and was never going to be: an assistive technology asking a
        // progress indicator for its value would have been told `A 5.5G, 30 GB, black · Apple
        // 25.1.3`. `ui/rail.slint`'s own bar answers `root.e.measure`, and `rail-line` is the same
        // sentence at bench scale — real bytes, `4.1 MB of 6.5 MB`, which is what §9.2 asks the
        // bench to mirror in one line.
        accessible-value: root.rail-line;
        accessible-value-minimum: 0;
        accessible-value-maximum: 1;
    }

    // **Declared LAST, and that is the whole of it working.** Slint paints siblings in
    // declaration order, so this sat FIRST and `well` — a full-area Rectangle at (0,0) covering
    // exactly where this is positioned — painted straight over it. The comment below said *over
    // the well*; the code put it under. The operator's report is the consequence: *"I cannot
    // trigger sidebar with hamburger click"* — a control that was there, correct, and invisible.
    //
    // Last rather than merely after `well`, because `short` covers the same corner in the
    // too-short state, which is the one state this control has to survive.
    //
    // **The drawer needs a control on the bench, and until §7.5's shelf went it had two.**
    // `MENU ›` and the write-target row both opened it; `Control + \` was the only other route.
    // Deleting the shelf without this would have left the drawer reachable by keyboard alone —
    // the same defect as a row drawn over a page with no slot: a surface you can see and cannot
    // get to. Which is what it became anyway, by z-order rather than by omission.
    //
    // Top-trailing, over the well rather than inside it, so it survives the well being hidden at
    // a window too short to draw the device — the one state where you most need a way out.
    Act {
        x: parent.width - self.width - Metric.s3;
        y: Metric.s3;
        icon: "menu";
        label: "Menu";
        visible: !root.drawer-open;
        activated => {
            root.open-drawer();
        }
    }
}
